          <h3 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-navy-950 dark:text-white">
            6. Downside Protection: Buying Insurance Against Unpredictable Market Crashes
          </h3>
          <p class="text-sm sm:text-base text-navy-600 dark:text-navy-400 mt-1 leading-relaxed">
            Why severe market crashes occur far more often than simple charts suggest, and how to put a safety floor under your hard-earned wealth.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-6">
          <div class="lg:col-span-2 space-y-5 text-sm sm:text-base text-navy-800 dark:text-navy-200 leading-relaxed">
            <h4 class="text-base font-bold text-navy-950 dark:text-white pt-2">
              Why Rare Crashes Happen More Often Than You Think ("Fat Tails")
            </h4>
            <p>
              Standard textbook finance assumes that stock market returns follow a neat, symmetrical bell curve where extreme crashes are supposed to happen once every 100 years.
            </p>
            <p>
              In the real world, human greed and panic create "fat tails"—meaning severe market plunges happen with surprising regularity (such as 1992, 2000, 2008, and 2020).
            </p>

            <h4 class="text-base font-bold text-navy-950 dark:text-white pt-2">
              How a Downside Safety Floor Works
            </h4>
            <p>
              Just like you buy comprehensive car insurance not because you plan to crash, but because a crash could bankrupt you, large portfolios can use <strong>downside floor protection</strong> (either via put options or systematically moving funds into sovereign debt):
            </p>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1.5 text-xs sm:text-sm text-navy-700 dark:text-navy-300">
              <li><strong>You set a maximum loss limit:</strong> For example, "My portfolio can never drop by more than 10% from its peak."</li>
              <li><strong>You pay a small annual fee:</strong> Typically 1.0% to 1.5% a year in premium or lower yield.</li>
              <li><strong>In a massive crash:</strong> If the market plunges 45%, your insurance pays out, capping your total loss at strictly 10%.</li>
            </ul>

            <h4 class="text-base font-bold text-navy-950 dark:text-white pt-2">
              When Should You Protect Your Portfolio?
            </h4>
            <p>
              Tail protection is not necessary at every stage of life:
            </p>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1.5 text-xs sm:text-sm text-navy-700 dark:text-navy-300">
              <li><strong>In Years 1 to 10 (Young Accumulator):</strong> Do not buy downside protection. Your portfolio is still building and market drops allow you to buy cheap units through your monthly SIP.</li>
              <li><strong>In Years 15+ (Within 5 Years of Your Goal):</strong> When your target corpus is nearly full and you cannot afford a 40% crash right before retirement, shifting into safe government bonds or using downside floors gives you complete peace of mind.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

