          <h3 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-navy-950 dark:text-white">
            11. Direct Stock Picking vs. Mutual Funds: What Bypassing Funds Really Requires
          </h3>
          <p class="text-sm sm:text-base text-navy-600 dark:text-navy-400 mt-1 leading-relaxed">
            Can an individual bypass mutual funds entirely and invest directly in individual stocks? Yes—but you must assume the full responsibilities of a professional fund manager.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-6">
          <div class="lg:col-span-2 space-y-5 text-sm sm:text-base text-navy-800 dark:text-navy-200 leading-relaxed">
            <p>
              Investing in direct equities eliminates the annual 0.1% to 1.5% mutual fund expense ratio. However, direct stock ownership is not a passive pastime—it is an active business enterprise requiring hundreds of hours of forensic analysis and emotional discipline.
            </p>

            <h4 class="text-base font-bold text-navy-950 dark:text-white pt-2">
              The 4 Non-Negotiable Responsibilities of Direct Stock Ownership
            </h4>
            <div class="space-y-3 text-xs sm:text-sm">
              <div class="p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-navy-200 dark:border-navy-800 bg-white dark:bg-navy-950 space-y-1">
                <span class="font-bold text-navy-950 dark:text-white block">1. Forensic Financial Statement Analysis</span>
                <p class="text-navy-600 dark:text-navy-400">
                  You must read annual reports, quarterly earnings releases, and cash flow statements. You must verify if reported net profits (PAT) are actually translating into <strong>Operating Cash Flow (OCF)</strong>, or if management is disguising revenue through uncollected receivables and aggressive accounting.
                </p>
              </div>
              <div class="p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-navy-200 dark:border-navy-800 bg-white dark:bg-navy-950 space-y-1">
                <span class="font-bold text-navy-950 dark:text-white block">2. Capital Allocation & Economic Moat Assessment</span>
                <p class="text-navy-600 dark:text-navy-400">
                  Great businesses earn a high <strong>Return on Capital Employed (ROCE > 18%)</strong> and possess pricing power. You must continuously monitor whether competitors, regulatory changes, or technological shifts are eroding the company's competitive advantage.
                </p>
              </div>
              <div class="p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-navy-200 dark:border-navy-800 bg-white dark:bg-navy-950 space-y-1">
                <span class="font-bold text-navy-950 dark:text-white block">3. Position Sizing & Concentration Discipline</span>
                <p class="text-navy-600 dark:text-navy-400">
                  Retail investors frequently make the fatal mistake of putting 30% to 50% of their net worth into 2 or 3 favorite stocks. When an unexpected corporate governance scandal or industry disruption strikes, decades of savings can be wiped out in weeks.
                </p>
              </div>
              <div class="p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-navy-200 dark:border-navy-800 bg-white dark:bg-navy-950 space-y-1">
